From 614db4251b62c83600300c625e7cb2522493afef Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artyom Shalkhakov <artyom.shalkhakov@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 17:14:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] NSNumberFormatter: implement the settings macOS keeps
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The settings a number formatter's keyed archive holds, compared with what
macOS 15 does with each:

- nilSymbol did nothing, and zeroSymbol set ICU's zero digit, so that
  "zip" stood for every 0 in a number.  nilSymbol, zeroSymbol and
  notANumberSymbol are now the strings for nil, zero and NaN, the same
  as attributedStringForNil, ...ForZero and ...ForNotANumber, and read
  back when parsed.
- The positive and negative infinity symbols were ICU's one infinity
  symbol; they are apart, "+∞" and "-∞" unless set, and an
  infinity formats as its symbol.
- textAttributesForZero, ...ForNil, ...ForNotANumber,
  ...ForPositiveInfinity and ...ForNegativeInfinity did nothing; they are
  kept, and -attributedStringForObjectValue:withDefaultAttributes: lays
  them over the defaults, as it now does the positive and negative ones.
- partialStringValidationEnabled is kept.  macOS shows no effect of it in
  either -isPartialStringValid: method, so neither does this.
- roundingIncrement took only float and double numbers and was lost on a
  change of style.  multiplier is kept, nil unless set; one that is not a
  whole number scales the value before ICU formats it and after it
  parses, since ICU takes whole multipliers only.
- currencyCode was set as a symbol under the text attribute's key.
- positiveFormat and negativeFormat were kept but ignored with the 10.4
  behaviour; they are applied as ICU patterns, outlive a change of
  style, and read back as the patterns in use.
- Parsing checked neither minimum, maximum nor allowsFloats, and
  -getObjectValue:forString:errorDescription: and -numberFromString:
  took a string that only began with a number.  They now refuse what
  macOS refuses, with its messages and NSFormattingError.
- The ordinal, currency ISO code, currency plural and accounting styles.
- Attribute getters kept what ICU first gave, and a BOOL as 1 or 2, so
  after a change of locale or style a BOOL set to NO came back as YES,
  and the 0 fraction digits -init set stayed with every style (a decimal
  formatter showed no fraction).  Getters now give what was set, else
  what ICU gives; what was set is laid over the style; and a formatter
  without a style gets macOS's: no fraction digits, 42 integer digits,
  no grouping.

Tests/base/NSNumberFormatter/settings.m checks each against the values
macOS 15 gives, and that they survive a keyed archive; 53 of its 70
checks fail without the change.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
